# expkit: an A/B-testing engine, and what it found in a famous 14M-user ad experiment

## The story: SRM passed, but the platform says HOLD

Criteo's public ad-incrementality test (13,979,592 users) splits treatment/control at exactly
{{ R.datasets.datasets[0].details.treatment_ratio|f(5) }} / {{ (1 - R.datasets.datasets[0].details.treatment_ratio)|f(5) }}, so the
sample-ratio check passes (p = {{ S.srm_p|f(3) }}). A naive dashboard reports conversions
**{{ C.raw.rel_lift|spct }}** and says ship.

![Criteo imbalance](results/figures/criteo_imbalance.png)

1. **The arms are not exchangeable.** Pre-treatment features predict assignment: a classifier two-sample test on
   {{ H.balance.n_test|int }} held-out users gives AUC {{ H.balance.auc|f(5) }} (z = {{ H.balance["z_vs_0.5"]|f(0) }}), and
   a joint χ² on the 12 feature means is {{ S.balance.chi2|int }} on 12 df. Treatment share is flat at
   {{ B.deciles_by_prognostic_score[0].treatment_share_all|pct }} in deciles 1–8 of predicted visit propensity and rises to
   {{ B.deciles_by_prognostic_score[9].treatment_share_all|pct }} in the top decile (left panel).
2. **Two documented causes are possible, and they disagree about the fix.** The dataset paper (Diemert et al. 2021,
   §3) says each pooled advertiser test was re-sampled to 85% treatment, *and* that "non-uniform negative sampling on
   labels" was applied. Re-balancing residue would be fixed by adjusting for features. Sampling on the outcome would not:
   it only preserves odds ratios. The data fit neither story alone. Among *non-visitors* the treatment share still rises,
   from {{ B.explanations.observed_nonvisitor_share_deciles_1_8_mean|pct(2) }} to
   {{ B.explanations.observed_nonvisitor_share_top_decile|pct(2) }}, which pure outcome sampling cannot produce.
3. **So the size of the effect is not identified from the released data.** Feature-adjusted estimates of the
   conversion lift range from {{ S.robustness[3].rel_lift|spct }} (CUPAC) to {{ S.robustness[1].rel_lift|spct }}
   (CUPED). Re-weighting non-visitors ×1 to ×20, to undo an unknown negative-sampling rate, moves the post-stratified lift
   from {{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative[0]|spct }} to
   {{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative[1]|spct }} (right panel; visits:
   {{ B.sensitivity_interval.visit_poststrat_relative[0]|spct }} to {{ B.sensitivity_interval.visit_poststrat_relative[1]|spct }}).
   On the odds-ratio scale, which outcome-dependent sampling does not distort, the crude vs stratified
   (Mantel–Haenszel) gap is small for visits ({{ B.odds_ratios.visit.crude_or|f(3) }} vs
   {{ B.odds_ratios.visit.mantel_haenszel_or|f(3) }}) and moderate for conversions ({{ B.odds_ratios.conversion.crude_or|f(3) }} vs
   {{ B.odds_ratios.conversion.mantel_haenszel_or|f(3) }}).
4. **The direction is robust, the magnitude is not.** Every estimator and every sensitivity scenario says the effect is
   positive and significant. The scorecard therefore stops at rule R1b, **{{ S.verdict }}**: a failed balance check is
   treated like an SRM, not as a warning to switch estimators. The memo recommends keeping the campaign on while the
   assignment is investigated, and not budgeting on any single number ([decision memo](docs/decision_memo.md)).

A synthetic test (`tests/test_selection_on_outcome.py`) shows the mechanism. It randomizes a treatment, then keeps
only 20% of the non-converters. That creates exactly this kind of feature imbalance, X-adjustment misses the true lift
badly, and the Mantel–Haenszel odds ratio recovers it.

